+++
title = "Configurable Host and Machine Identifiers"
task = "TASK-00.02.02"
version = "0.2.2"
+++

# 1. Objective

Standardize the configuration identifiers used to identify the host configuration directory and valid machine definition directories.

These identifiers MUST be configurable through all existing configuration sources.

# 2. Parameters

Introduce the following parameters:

| Parameter | Type | Default value |
|---|---|---|
| id-host | String | iasi-host |
| id-machines | String | config-data |

- `id-host`: directory name identifying the host configuration.
- `id-machines`: subdirectory name identifying a valid machine definition.

These parameters represent directory identifiers, not absolute or relative paths.

# 3. Default configuration

Update the existing `defaults.toml` located at the root of the Java resources directory.

```toml
id-host = "iasi-host"
id-machines = "config-data"
```

Use these exact parameter names.

Remove the previous configuration keys for these same identifiers. Do not maintain duplicate keys or compatibility aliases.

Update the embedded Java default values accordingly.

# 4. External configuration

The external TOML configuration file specified through `--config` MUST support the same parameter names.

Example:

```toml
id-host = "my-host"
id-machines = "machine-data"
```

# 5. Environment variables

Both parameters MUST be configurable through environment variables.

Using `IASI` as the environment prefix:

```text
IASI_ID_HOST
IASI_ID_MACHINES
```

Environment variable names MUST be generated according to the following general rules:

- Use the configured environment prefix.
- Convert parameter names to uppercase.
- Replace hyphens with underscores.
- Use an underscore as the prefix separator.

The environment prefix MUST follow the existing `--env` configuration mechanism.

This transformation MUST be generic. Do not implement parameter-specific environment variable mappings.

# 6. Command-line interface

Introduce the following CLI parameters:

```text
--id-host <identifier>
--id-machines <identifier>
```

Include both parameters in the application help, including their default values and descriptions.

Preserve the existing help formatting conventions.

# 7. Configuration precedence

Preserve the established configuration precedence, from lowest to highest priority:

1. Embedded Java default values.
2. Internal resource `defaults.toml`.
3. External configuration file specified through `--config`.
4. Environment variables.
5. Command-line arguments.

Each configuration source MUST be able to override the values provided by lower-priority sources.

# 8. Execution context and integration

Both resolved identifiers MUST be available through the existing execution context.

Replace hardcoded directory identifiers in the existing Java implementation with the corresponding resolved configuration values.

Machine discovery MUST use `id-machines` to identify valid machine definition directories.

Host configuration discovery MUST use `id-host` wherever applicable.

Preserve the existing semantics and default values of `--definitions` and `--machines`.

Both MUST remain `.` by default.

# 9. Corporate requirements

- Follow existing IASI corporate development guidelines.
- Reuse existing configuration mechanisms.
- Reuse existing argument-processing mechanisms.
- Reuse the existing execution context.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not modify platform-specific scripts.
- Do not implement new host orchestration functionality.
- Do not refactor unrelated packages.

# 10. Validation

Verify only the functionality directly affected by this task:

- Successful compilation.
- Embedded default values.
- Internal TOML configuration.
- External TOML configuration.
- Environment variable configuration.
- Command-line configuration.
- Configuration precedence.
- Machine discovery using a customized identifier.
- Updated help information.

Do not execute the complete test suite or corporate Quality Gate.

# 11. Version

Update the application version to `0.2.2`.

# 12. Acceptance criteria

Both identifiers are configurable through all established configuration sources, using consistent naming conventions and the existing configuration precedence.

No duplicate configuration keys or hardcoded directory identifiers remain in the affected Java implementation.